---
title: Find CI optimizations with an AI agent
description: Have an AI agent find costly flaky tasks, tasks to split, cache misses, idle workers and credit trends in your Nx Cloud CI data, on demand or on a schedule.
filter: 'type:Guides'
topics:
  - 'Nx Cloud'
  - 'Continuous integration'
---

The `ci-optimization` skill teaches an AI agent where to look in your recorded Nx Cloud CI data, and how
to rank what it finds by the CI time or credits it costs.
It reads data only: what to do with a finding (a report, an issue, a pull request) is up to you and your
prompt.

| Finding                       | What the agent looks at                                                                |
| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Flaky tasks                   | Daily flakiness and reruns per task, ranked by CI time lost, with failing-attempt logs |
| Tasks to atomize or shard     | Long test and e2e targets that run in most pipelines and aren't split yet              |
| Cache misses and broad inputs | Targets ranked by hours spent on cache misses, and the likely cause                    |
| Worker utilization            | Worker startup, busy and idle time, CPU and memory per launch template                 |
| Compute and credits           | Credits and VM minutes per pipeline, compared between periods                          |

The data comes from the [Nx Cloud Public API](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/public-api) and the Nx Cloud
CLI.
For causes the API doesn't record, such as a target's inputs, the agent reads your workspace.

## Install the skill

The skill is part of the `nx-cloud` plugin for coding agents.

For Claude Code:

```shell
claude plugin marketplace add https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nrwl/nx-ai-agents-config/main/plugins/.claude-plugin/marketplace.json
claude plugin install nx-cloud@nx-plugins
```

For Codex:

```shell
codex plugin marketplace add nrwl/nx-ai-agents-config
codex plugin add nx-cloud@nx-plugins
```

The agent needs Public API access to your workspace: your login from `npx nx login`, or a CI access token
in `NX_CLOUD_ACCESS_TOKEN`.
See [Authenticate with the Nx Cloud Public API](/docs/kb/authenticate-public-api).
Some commands it uses need `NX_CLOUD_EXPERIMENTAL_CLI=true` in its environment.

## Ask for a review

Run your agent from the workspace root, and ask in your own words:

```plaintext
Which flaky tasks cost us the most CI time last week, and why do they fail?
```

```plaintext
Which test targets should we atomize or shard?
```

```plaintext
Find targets with a low cache hit rate on main this month and check whether their inputs are too broad.
```

```plaintext
Are our Nx Cloud workers sized right? Compare credits per pipeline with the previous two weeks.
```

Treat each finding as a hypothesis until a change is measured in CI.
The report links the pipelines and runs it is based on.

## Run it on a schedule

Any scheduler that can run your agent headless works: a CI cron job,
[Claude Code routines](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/routines), or
[Codex automations](https://developers.openai.com/codex/app/automations).
Give the job a read-only CI access token as `NX_CLOUD_ACCESS_TOKEN`.
On a single-tenant installation, also set `NX_CLOUD_API` to your Nx Cloud URL.

This GitHub Actions workflow writes a weekly report to the job summary:

```yaml {% title=".github/workflows/ci-optimization.yml" %}
name: Weekly CI optimization report

on:
  schedule:
    - cron: '0 6 * * 1'
  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  report:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 22
      - run: npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code
      - run: |
          claude plugin marketplace add https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nrwl/nx-ai-agents-config/main/plugins/.claude-plugin/marketplace.json
          claude plugin install nx-cloud@nx-plugins
      - name: Write the report
        env:
          ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: ${{ secrets.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY }}
          NX_CLOUD_ACCESS_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.NX_CLOUD_READ_TOKEN }}
          NX_CLOUD_EXPERIMENTAL_CLI: 'true'
        run: |
          claude -p "Report the flaky tasks that cost the most CI time over the last 7 full UTC days, with the likely cause of the top 3. Output Markdown only." \
            --allowedTools "Bash Read Grep Glob" > report.md
          cat report.md >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
```

To post the report somewhere else, or to open an issue or a pull request for the top finding, say so in
the prompt and give the job the permissions and credentials that needs.

## Example: a weekly flaky-task report

This is part of a report the skill produced for the Nx repository itself, for one week in November 2026:

```markdown
## Flaky tasks (time lost = reruns × the file's mean cache-miss duration)

| Target             | Reruns | Days flaky | Time lost | Worst files (reruns)                     |
| ------------------ | ------ | ---------- | --------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| e2e-release:e2e-ci | 643    | 6/7        | 34.5 h    | preserve-local-dependency-protocols (80) |
| e2e-maven:e2e-ci   | 74     | 6/7        | 16.2 h    | maven.test.ts (45)                       |
| e2e-nx:e2e-ci      | 82     | 5/7        | 15.9 h    | affected-graph (37), watch (18)          |

Nearly all reruns passed on retry, so CI ends green but still pays the time.

- e2e-release, preserve-local-dependency-protocols.test.ts: 3 failed attempts, each a
  different test hitting Jest's `Exceeded timeout of 120000 ms` after 228 to 330 s.
  Hypothesis: package installs sometimes run past the 120 s `testTimeout`.
- e2e-maven, maven.test.ts: every failure was the non-batch build hitting its own
  15-minute timeout, so each flake costs the full 15 minutes before the retry.
```
